// The Appeal, ch5: What She Saw. Saturday, February 13, 2027 (one day, morning on Federal to night in the basement).
// Coverage per the bible: scenes LR09x3 LR10x4 LR11 RC06x2 RC05 RC04; quiz LR09x3 LR10x4 LR11 LR06 LR18 LR08 LR07 RC06x2 RC05 RC04x2 RC07.
// Coverage pass 2026-10-25 (quiz now 20): Q19 RC08 on p2, Q20 long LR10.
// Full-length passage (p2): Gladstone's 1858 count of Homer's color words, the nineteenth century claim that color vision had evolved, Berlin and Kay's 1969 basic color terms, and the step from vocabulary to vision; humanities, written fresh.
// Paired case passage (p1, depth pass 2026-11-06): Consuelo's 2007 statement as typed into Gene's report, with no cap and no boy, matching his struck out cap and his margin boy? (Passage A) and Dani's notes of her account at the kitchen table, February 13, 2027 (Passage B), about 470 words with five questions (RC14x2 RC05 RC07 RC04). It replaces the short Archuleta transcript; the ditch walk now lives in Consuelo's lines in f5s8. Quiz swaps the same day: Q6 is a conditional chain (LR09) and Q7 a counting word flaw (LR06), both taught in the new lesson section Chains and counting words; the LR18, LR08 and LR07 spaced review items and two LR10 items were cut to make room. Quiz codes now LR09x4 LR10x3 LR11 LR06x2 RC14x2 RC05 RC07 RC04x3 RC06x2 RC08. New canon (approved 2026-11-06): Shannon's October pennants on the light pole at the curb (f5s8).
// Weekdays computed 2026-10-16: Wednesday, February 3; Wednesday, February 10; Thursday, February 11; Saturday, February 13, 2027 (17 weeks before Saturday, June 12). Saturday, October 27, 2007; Monday, June 2, 2008.
// Reveal schedule: Consuelo gives the cap and the boy crying "Papi" at about 10:50; "They used my words too, mija." The one look out her window (ditch, tracks, no through road) settles ch4's hypothetical. The ledconsuelo flag sits on the height question in f5s6.
// Ends on something Dani does (bible ch4 calls, 2026-10-16): the old washer on the curb, Teo's text, and Dani running the first load.
// All questions, passages and answer choices are original. Not affiliated with or endorsed by LSAC.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 5, part: `Part Two: The Witnesses`, title: `What She Saw`, weeks: `February 2027`,
  tasks: [`LR06`,`LR09`,`LR10`,`LR11`,`RC04`,`RC05`,`RC06`,`RC07`,`RC08`,`RC14`],
  cast: [
    [`Consuelo Baca`,`The eyewitness, 83. Still lives in the house across the street from the old lot. Saw a man in a red jacket at a quarter past eleven and never saw his face.`],
    [`Alma Baca`,`Consuelo's granddaughter, 27. A third grade teacher in Commerce City. She was eight that night, the girl with the earache.`],
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard. Wrote to Consuelo in January and didn't get invited.`],
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, at Sterling. Calls Wednesdays at seven.`],
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 29. Has a key to the house and a shop vac in the basement.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Saturday, February 13. Consuelo Baca had called Ana's office three times on Tuesday and left three messages on the machine. Ana played them for Dani in order, standing by the desk with her arms folded, as if they were exhibits.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"This is Consuelo Baca. I'll talk to the girl. Saturday, after the four o'clock Mass."</span> A click. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Consuelo Baca again. Not after. Before. Two o'clock. My granddaughter says before."</span> Then the third one, after a cough: <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Tell her come hungry."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"I wrote her in January,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Letterhead. My name, my bar number, the works. She called back and asked for the girl. So you go alone, and you do the hardest thing there is to do with a witness."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Which is?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nothing. Ask her what she saw. Then shut up. Write down her words, not yours."</span>`,
    `Seventeen weeks to June. On Wednesday, February 3, Dani had made her move at last, and it wasn't the pawn. She'd brought a rook across instead, the long way, and on the other end of the line her father went so quiet she thought the call had dropped. <span class="said" data-who="Luis" data-tone="Quiet, slow, taken aback.">"Huh,"</span> he'd said. Last Wednesday he moved and she moved, and neither of them said a word about the bishop.`,
    `Thursday she'd told Teo on the phone where she'd be on Saturday. Across from the old lot. He'd said okay. Then he'd asked whether the floor down there was still wet, and she'd said it was fine, and he'd said it wasn't.`,
    `Dicta was asleep on the rug with her legs straight out, running somewhere. Ana stepped over her to get to the filing cabinet.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: what follows`,
    sections: [
      { h: `No argument`,
        body: [`February on Federal. The dryers downstairs have been going since six, and the floor's warm enough that Dicta has given up the rug for the bare boards by the radiator. Pencil.`,
          `For four weeks I've handed you arguments. Somebody concluded something, and you found the conclusion and went after the step that got there. Tonight there's no step to go after. A lot of these questions give you a short passage with no conclusion in it at all. Just facts. A schedule, a rule, a few things somebody counted. The question asks what those facts let you say.`,
          `So you're the one drawing the conclusion now. Which means you're the one who can overreach.`,
          `There are two strengths of this question, and the stem tells you which. Must be true means the facts prove it. Not suggest it, prove it: if every statement in the passage is true, the answer can't be false. Most strongly supported is a notch softer. The answer doesn't have to be guaranteed. It has to be the one the facts back best, usually by a short step, and the other four will be either unsupported or too big.`,
          `Most of the time the right answer joins two facts. Every pilot at the flight school holds a medical certificate. Everyone who holds one has to see the doctor once a year. Tomás flies for the school. Nobody wrote down that Tomás sees the doctor every year, but it's sitting right there, and that's the answer. Look for a fact that hooks onto another one.`],
        terms: [[`Must be true`,`A claim the stated facts guarantee. If every statement in the passage is true, this can't be false.`],[`Most strongly supported`,`The claim the stated facts back best, usually by one short step, even if it isn't guaranteed.`]],
        exam: `Must be true stems say that if the statements above are true, which of the following must also be true. Supported stems ask which choice the statements most strongly support, or which is most strongly supported by the information. Neither stem asks you to find a flaw or fix anything. Take the facts as given and draw from them.` },
      { h: `The big ones`,
        body: [`The traps here are the opposite of the traps in a flaw question. There, you were told the argument was bad and the wrong answers defended it. Here, the facts are fine and the wrong answers say more than the facts do.`,
          `Watch the size of the words. All, always, never, only, the reason, proves. If the facts say most, the answer can't say all. If the facts say one thing happened after another, the answer can't say because. A choice can be likely, even true in the real world, and still go one step past the page. That one is wrong.`,
          `Watch the arrows. If every pilot at the school holds a certificate, that doesn't mean everyone with a certificate flies for the school. You know this from the flaw chapter. Here the reversed rule shows up as an answer choice that sounds exactly like a fact you were given.`,
          `And watch the counting words. Most and most can do something nobody expects. If more than half of a choir sings at the early service and more than half of the same choir sings at the late one, then at least one singer does both, because two groups that each take more than half of the same room have to overlap. Some plus some gives you nothing. Most plus most of the same group gives you some.`,
          `Could be true is its own trap. A choice that nothing in the passage rules out is still not a choice the passage proves. On a must be true question, could be true is wrong. It's only right when the stem asks for something that could be true.`],
        terms: [[`Could be true`,`A claim nothing in the passage rules out. Not enough for a must be true question.`],[`Overlap`,`When two groups each make up more than half of the same whole, at least one member belongs to both.`]],
        exam: `Expect a choice that would be true if one more fact were added. Ask what you had to bring with you to make it work. If the answer is anything at all, cross it off.` },
      { h: `Can't be`,
        body: [`The rarest of these asks the question upside down. Which one of the following cannot be true? Or, which could be true, except? Both are asking for the one choice the facts rule out.`,
          `Test each choice against the facts and ask one thing: does it contradict them? Not, is it unlikely. Unlikely things happen every day. A choice that's merely improbable could be true, so it isn't your answer. You want the one that can't stand next to the facts without one of them falling down.`,
          `Rules make this easy, if you know what a rule lets you do. If A, then B. You get one move for free: no B, so no A. If every dog in the kennel has had its shots, then a dog without its shots isn't in the kennel. Say a choice puts an unvaccinated dog in the kennel. That one can't be true.`,
          `Two accounts can work the same way. Put two people's stories side by side and ask what they can't both allow. Sometimes the answer is the thing everybody's fighting about.`],
        terms: [[`Contrapositive`,`The one move a rule gives you for free: if A always brings B, then no B means no A.`],[`Cannot be true`,`A claim that contradicts the stated facts. Merely unlikely isn't enough.`]],
        exam: `These stems may ask which choice cannot be true, which must be false, or which could be true except one. Rewrite the except form in your head as cannot be true before you read the choices.` },
      { h: `Chains and counting words`,
        body: [`Rules link. If a man was at that curb at a quarter past eleven, he was near the lot. If he was near the lot then, he hadn't gone home before eleven. If he hadn't gone home before eleven, your father's story is wrong. Join the links, the end of one to the start of the next, and you get one long rule: at the curb means the story's wrong. The long rule has a contrapositive like any short one. If the story's right, he wasn't at the curb. That's the whole fight in one line, and the State never had to write it down.`,
          `The test hides its arrows in words. If, every, all and any point from the first idea to the second. Only if points at whatever comes after it: you sit at counsel table only if you're on the case means at the table, so on the case. Unless means if not: no testing unless the judge orders it means no order, no testing. No A is a B runs both directions. No juror is a witness, so no witness is a juror.`,
          `Two moves feel like logic and aren't. Turning the arrow around: every man on the crew wore the red jacket, but a man in a red jacket isn't therefore on the crew. Half of Denver was in red that night. And denying both sides: not on the crew doesn't mean not in red. The wrong answers on these questions are mostly those two moves, dressed up to sound like the rule you were given.`,
          `Then the counting words. Some means at least one. Could be one, could be all. It never means not all. Most means more than half, and that's it. Some plus some gets you nothing. Most plus most of the same group gets you some. Most of the bar in red, most of the bar from Boston: somebody in red was from Boston, because two halves of one room can't miss each other. Now the trap. Most of the bar was in red, and most of the red was Boston. You'd think that makes most of the bar Boston. It doesn't. That second most only counts the people in red, and that's a smaller room. So every time two counting words meet, I want you to ask, out of how many?`,
          `Now the harder translations, one step at a time. Step one is and and or with a not in front. Not both means at least one fails. He wasn't both at the bar and at home at ten: fine, he was in one place at most, maybe neither. Neither means both fail. He was neither at the bar nor at home: he was somewhere else. People swap those two all the time. Say them out loud until they're dull. Not both, at least one is false. Neither, both are false.`,
          `Step two is a rule with two parts on one side. If a man was on the crew and wore red, he was in the lot. Flip it and negate it, and the and turns to or. If he wasn't in the lot, then he wasn't on the crew or he didn't wear red. One of them failed. You don't get to say which, and that's where the points go. It works the other way too. If he was at the curb or at the bar, somebody saw him. Nobody saw him, so he wasn't at the curb and he wasn't at the bar. Both. An or on the front of a rule becomes an and when you flip it.`,
          `Step three is the hard words. Unless: cross it out, write if not, and that's the front of the arrow. The motion fails unless the lab finds a second profile means no second profile, the motion fails. Not the other way round. The only: the only people who may touch the exhibit are counsel means may touch it, so counsel. It doesn't say all counsel may touch it. The common error on all of these is running the arrow both ways because the sentence sounds fair. Here's one for your card. Everyone who testified was sworn, and nobody who was sworn lied. Join them, then flip the chain. If you wrote that anyone who lied didn't testify, you've got it.`],
        terms: [[`Chain`,`Conditionals linked end to start, so the first condition leads all the way to the last result.`],[`Only if`,`Marks what's needed. A only if B means if A, then B.`],[`Unless`,`Means if not. A unless B means if not B, then A.`],[`Some`,`At least one, and possibly all.`]],
        exam: `Chains turn up in must be true, sufficient assumption and parallel questions. Rewrite each rule as an arrow before you combine them, then check every choice for an arrow that's been turned around or denied.` },
      { h: `Reading between`,
        body: [`Reading Comprehension asks the same questions in different clothes. A detail question asks what the passage says. An inference question asks what it suggests, or implies, or what can be inferred. The second is the supported question from Logical Reasoning, with a longer passage.`,
          `The right inference is almost never far from the text. Usually it's a line from the passage said in other words, plus one small step. If you have to argue for an answer, it's probably too far. If you can put your finger on the line it comes from and say the step in five words, it's probably right.`,
          `One kind of inference is about what isn't there. A transcript is a list of questions somebody chose to ask. Read the questions, and you can often say what nobody asked. That's an inference, and a strong one, because the page is the whole record. You're not guessing what the witness knew. You're reading what she was given a chance to say.`,
          `And keep the voices apart, same as last time. What a witness said, what the officer wrote down, and what somebody else concluded from both are three different things, and an answer choice will try to hand you one dressed as another.`],
        terms: [[`Inference`,`A claim a passage supports without stating it, reached by a short step from what it does say.`]],
        exam: `Inference stems use words like suggests, implies, and can be inferred. Detail stems use according to the passage, or the passage states. Before you answer, find the line, and decide whether the question wants that line or one step past it.` },
      { h: `The whole table`,
        body: [`You've got the arrows. Now I want them fast, because on the hard questions the arrow is buried under a not, an and, an or and an unless, and the clock doesn't care that you're still translating. So here's every piece, one at a time, and then we stack them.`,
          `Start with denying, because everything else leans on it. To deny a sentence, ask what the world looks like if it's false, and say the smallest thing that makes it false. All the tenants signed the lease. False means at least one didn't. Not that none of them did. That's far more than you need. Some tenant signed. False means no tenant did. Most of the tenants signed. False means half or fewer did. Always becomes not always. Never becomes at least once. Put three lines on a card and carry it a week. All goes to some not. Some goes to none. Most goes to half or fewer.`,
          `Then and and or. Deny an and, and you get an or. It's false that the clerk was early and polite means she wasn't early, or she wasn't polite, or both. Not late and rude. Those are opposites, and an opposite isn't a denial. A clerk who walks in right on time, perfectly polite, makes the sentence false, and she isn't late or rude. Deny an or, and you get an and. It's false that the van was at the shop or in the lot means it wasn't at the shop and it wasn't in the lot. The not walks inside and the joining word flips. People get this one backward more than any other, and the test knows it. When a not crosses an and, the and turns to or. When it crosses an or, the or turns to and.`,
          `Now deny a rule. If it rains, the game's canceled. When is that rule false? Not when it stays dry. The rule says nothing about dry days. It's false only if it rains and they play anyway. So the denial of if A then B is A without B: a case where the front happens and the back doesn't. That's what a counterexample is, and it's why one rainy game played sinks the whole rule. What people want to say is the opposite rule, if it rains they play. Too strong. You only need the one game.`,
          `Next, the words that build arrows. Some words put what follows them on the front of the arrow. If, when, whenever, every, all, any, each, anyone who. Others put what follows them on the back. Only if, only when, requires, needs, depends on, must have, is necessary for. If you sit for the test, you registered. You sit for the test only if you registered. Sitting requires registering. Three sentences, one arrow: sit, then registered.`,
          `Only by itself works the same way as only if: what follows it goes on the back of the arrow. Only members may vote means vote, then member. Now set it next to all members may vote, which means member, then may vote. Same words, arrows pointing opposite ways. The only works the other way around. The only people who may vote are members means the same arrow, vote, then member, but this time the words right after the only, people who may vote, are the front. So carry two rules. Only, alone, marks the back. The only marks the front.`,
          `Unless means if not, and the if not goes in front. You can't park here unless you have a permit. No permit, then you can't park. Flip it: parked here legally, then you had a permit. Without works the same: no entry without a ticket means no ticket, then no entry. Until often does too. The pool stays closed until the lifeguard comes means no lifeguard yet, then closed. One warning. In ordinary talk unless sounds like it runs both ways, as if a permit guarantees you a spot. On the test it doesn't. The permit gets you past this one rule. There could be another sign about street sweeping on Tuesdays.`,
          `No A unless B gets its own line, because the double negative slows everybody down and principle questions love it. No one may board unless they have a ticket. Turn the no one into a person may not. A person may not board unless they have a ticket. Unless is if not, so no ticket means no boarding. Flip it, and anyone who boarded had a ticket. Short form: no A unless B is A, then B. Learn it as one block and stop working it out every time.`,
          `Two parts on one side stack the same way denial does. If you're a member and you've paid, you vote. Flip it, and the and turns to or: didn't vote, so you're not a member or you haven't paid. If you're a member, you vote or you send a proxy. Flip that one: didn't vote and didn't send a proxy, so not a member. Two pieces joined by and on the back can be split into two rules. If member, then vote and pay dues means if member, vote, and if member, pay dues. An or on the back can't be split. You only know that one of them happened.`,
          `Here's one with every piece in it. No applicant who lacks a reference will be interviewed unless they've worked here before. Peel it slowly the first time. The no and the unless work together: an applicant who lacks a reference and hasn't worked here won't be interviewed. Two pieces in front, joined by and. Flip it, and the and turns to or: anyone who was interviewed had a reference, or had worked here before, or both. Now ask it things. Jo was interviewed and has never worked here. She had a reference. Sam has a reference. Nothing follows. The rule never says what happens to people who have one. That empty spot is where the wrong answer lives, sounding sure of itself.`,
          `Last, the rule that runs both ways. If and only if, or exactly when. Members vote if and only if they've paid means two arrows: paid, then vote, and vote, then paid. You'll rarely see it said in those words, and when you do, both arrows are yours. When you don't, you get one, however fair the second one sounds.`,
          `To make it fast, drill it the boring way. Every night for two weeks, take three rules from anything, a lease, a school handbook, the sign on the bus, and do four things to each one. Write the arrow. Write the flip. Say what the rule doesn't tell you. Deny it. Ten minutes a night. By the end you'll stop translating. You'll just see it.`],
        terms: [[`Negation`,`The smallest claim that makes a statement false. All goes to some not, some goes to none, most goes to half or fewer.`],[`No A unless B`,`Means if A, then B. No one boards unless they have a ticket: boarded, so had a ticket.`],[`If and only if`,`A rule that runs both ways, so both arrows hold. Rare, and you only get it when the words say so.`]],
        exam: `Conditional words cluster in must be true, sufficient assumption, flaw and principle questions. On a hard one, write the arrow and its flip in the margin before you read the choices. Then each choice is a match test: the arrow, the flip, or one of the two moves that only look like them.` },
      { h: `An inference, worked`,
        body: [`One reading inference, slowly. A passage about a river town says the ferry stopped running in 1931, the year the bridge opened, and that the ferryman's family opened a store on the far bank that same year. The question asks what can be inferred.`,
          `The choices, the way they'd come. The bridge put the ferry out of business. Likely, and too far. The passage gives you two events in one year and never says one caused the other. The ferryman's family had lived on the far bank before 1931. Not stated, and nothing points to it. In the years before 1931, someone crossing the river there could have used the ferry. That's one short step: the ferry ran until 1931, so in those years it was there to use. That's the inference. The store did better than the ferry had. Nothing compares them. The town grew after the bridge opened. Not in the passage at all.`,
          `Notice that the right answer is the dullest one. It's nearly a restatement, with one small step. The two that tempt you are the ones that tell a story, the bridge killing the ferry and the family moving. Stories are what the passage left out. When an inference choice makes you think and that's why, ask where the because is written. If it isn't, the choice is reaching.`],
        terms: [[`Dull is right`,`On inference questions, the right answer is usually close to a line in the passage, with one small step past it.`]],
        exam: `Inference stems say the passage suggests, implies, or most strongly supports something. Find the line, take one small step, and drop any choice that needs a cause or a motive the passage never states.` }
    ],
    video: [`1A1frX9DxuU`,`Using a Must Be True Mindset on LR`,`LSAT Demon`,`9:48`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f5s1`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC05`, title:`Eleven seventeen`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana came back from the cabinet with the dispatch log in its plastic sleeve and laid it on the desk next to Gene's notebook, still zipped in its freezer bag. She put one finger on the second line and kept it there.`,
        `<prop say="Dispatch log, Saturday, October 27, 2007, page two. Eleven seventeen and thirty eight seconds: 911 call received. The caller, C. Baca, lives across from the rear lot of the Night Owl Tap, on Highway 2. Eleven seventeen and fifty two seconds: the caller says a male, red jacket, ball cap, struck the victim with an unknown object, and the subject fled on foot. Eleven eighteen and twenty seconds: the victim is down at the curb, not moving. Medical requested. Eleven eighteen and forty one seconds: unit two fourteen en route." type="doc" title="Dispatch log, Saturday, October 27, 2007, page 2">2317:38 911 CALL RCVD. RP C BACA, RES ACROSS FROM REAR LOT, NIGHT OWL TAP, HWY 2.<br>2317:52 RP ADV MALE RED JKT BALL CAP STRUCK VICT W UNK OBJ, SUBJ FLED ON FOOT.<br>2318:20 VICT DOWN AT CURB, NOT MOVING. MEDICAL REQUESTED.<br>2318:41 UNIT 214 EN ROUTE.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Before you sit in that woman's kitchen, you know to the word what she already said. Otherwise she'll tell you something old and you'll think it's new. Or she'll tell you something new and you'll miss it. What does this line state? Not what it means. What it says."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the caller reported a man in a red jacket and a ball cap hitting someone and running off on foot.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`A man in a red jacket and a ball cap hit somebody and ran off on foot, Dani reads. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's it. That's the whole line."</span> Ana lifts her finger. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Red jacket. Ball cap. Struck. On foot. Four things, at eleven seventeen, from a woman with a sick kid and a phone in her hand. Anything she tells you today, check it against those four."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. The line says the caller reported a male in a red jacket and ball cap who struck the victim with an unknown object and fled on foot. That's all four facts and nothing added. It records what she said, not that it happened.`},
        {t:`That the man in the cap hit Travis with something heavy, like a pipe, and then ran off from the curb.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Unknown object,"</span> Ana reads. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Unknown. Not heavy, not a pipe. You furnished it. Half your sentence is on the page and the other half came from you."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. Partly right. The log does say he struck the victim and fled. It says the object was unknown. Heavy and pipe are additions, the most common way a detail answer goes wrong.`},
        {t:`That the man in the cap ran north from the curb, toward the ditch and the railroad tracks behind it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"North is in Gene's notebook,"</span> Ana says, tapping the freezer bag. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not here. This says on foot. And there's no ditch on this page. You brought that with you."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. Wrong source. Gene's notebook says the man ran north. The log says only that he fled on foot, and neither one mentions a ditch.`},
        {t:`That the caller saw the man's face clearly enough but didn't know who he was by name at the time.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Where's the face?"</span> Ana says. Dani looks. There isn't one. The log never mentions a face at all, and every other paper says she never saw one.`,
          why:`Specific detail. Not stated, and contradicted elsewhere in the record. The log says nothing about a face.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s2`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR09`, title:`Twenty three minutes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana pulled Ronnie's statement from Phil's box and set it under the log, so the two times lined up. Eleven seventeen at the top. Eleven forty at the bottom.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Here's what I've got. Of every police paper we have, Ronnie's is the first one with your father's name on it. Twenty to twelve. The log is eleven seventeen. Take both times as right."</span> She held up the pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Must be true. Not likely. Not probably. Must. What does that give you?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"She said cap at 11:17, twenty three minutes before Pa's name is on any police paper we have."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So on paper, the cap comes first,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Before anybody wrote his name down, she'd already said cap. When she said cap there wasn't a name on any paper for it to help. That's not a probably. That's arithmetic. Why she said it is a different question."</span> She writes 11:17 BEFORE 11:40 under CAP on the board, small.`,
          why:`Must be true. Join the two facts. The cap is in the log at eleven seventeen, and the name first appears at eleven forty. So on these papers the cap came first. Nothing is added and nothing is guessed.`},
        {t:`"The man who hit Travis really did have a ball cap on his head that night, whatever else he wore."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Probably,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She said so, fast, before anybody had a reason to want it. But it was dark, and she was across a street. She could've seen a hood. Probably isn't must."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. Likely, and not guaranteed. The log proves she said cap. It doesn't prove she was right about it.`},
        {t:`"Pa couldn't have been the man she saw. Pa never once wore a hat in his whole life."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now you sound like me in a brief,"</span> Ana says, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"and I lose briefs. Never wore one isn't couldn't. And it isn't on either of these pages."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. Too strong, and imported. Never wore one comes from Ronnie and from you, not from these two papers. A hat goes on a head once.`},
        {t:`"She told Gene about the cap again on Sunday morning, at her house, when he came over to see her."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"True,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Freezer bag, though. Not this desk. Two times, I said. Two times don't get you to Sunday."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. Happens to be true, from the notebook. Doesn't come out of the log or the statement. If you had to go fetch it from another page, it isn't the answer.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC06`, title:`The stand`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Then Ana took a page out of Phil's trial binder and laid it beside the freezer bag. Gene's notebook on the left. The trial on the right.`,
        `<prop say="Trial transcript, Monday, June 2, 2008, direct and cross examination of Consuelo Baca. Question: Mrs. Baca, where do you live? Answer: Across the street from the Night Owl. Behind it, across from the back lot. Question: Were you awake at about a quarter past eleven on the night of Saturday, October 27, 2007? Answer: Yes. My granddaughter had an earache. I was at the kitchen sink. Question: What did you see? Answer: A man hit another man by the curb, with something in his hand. The man fell down. The other one ran. Question: What was the man who ran wearing? Answer: A red jacket. Question: Did you see his face? Answer: No. I never saw his face. Question: What did you do? Answer: I called 911. Thank you, Mrs. Baca. Cross examination by Mr. Abrams. Question: Mrs. Baca, you can't tell this jury who that man was, can you? Answer: No, sir. Nothing further." type="doc" title="Trial transcript, Monday, June 2, 2008, direct and cross examination of Consuelo Baca">Q. Mrs. Baca, where do you live?<br>A. Across the street from the Night Owl. Behind it, across from the back lot.<br>Q. Were you awake at about a quarter past eleven on the night of Saturday, October 27, 2007?<br>A. Yes. My granddaughter had an earache. I was at the kitchen sink.<br>Q. What did you see?<br>A. A man hit another man by the curb, with something in his hand. The man fell down. The other one ran.<br>Q. What was the man who ran wearing?<br>A. A red jacket.<br>Q. Did you see his face?<br>A. No. I never saw his face.<br>Q. What did you do?<br>A. I called 911.<br>Q. Thank you, Mrs. Baca.<br>CROSS EXAMINATION BY MR. ABRAMS<br>Q. Mrs. Baca, you can't tell this jury who that man was, can you?<br>A. No, sir.<br>Q. Nothing further.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Seven questions, counting Phil's,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read it against Gene's page. What did she tell somebody in 2007 that this page tells you nobody asked her in 2008?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`She was never asked whether the man had anything on besides the red jacket.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`What was the man who ran wearing? A red jacket. And the next question is about his face. Nobody asked, anything else? She'd said cap to dispatch, and cap to Gene, and the State had stopped one word short of it. So had Phil.`,
          why:`Inference. One open clothing question, one answer, and no follow up. Nobody asked "anything else?", so the cap she gave dispatch and Gene never came up. The transcript never says so. Read the questions and it's there.`},
        {t:`She was never asked how she could make out the jacket's color from across the street.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Sure,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Phil should've. Streetlight, distance, all of it. Did she say one word about light to anybody in 2007, though? No. I asked what she said then that nobody let her say now."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Fair, as far as it goes: nothing on the page touches light or distance. Wrong target. The question was about something she'd told police in 2007.`},
        {t:`She was asked about the cap on the stand and forgot to mention it in her answer.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Dani looks for the question. It isn't there. Nobody on this page says the word cap. You can't forget to answer a question nobody asked you.`,
          why:`Inference. Contradicted. No question in the transcript mentions a cap or a hat.`},
        {t:`She was never asked whether she'd seen the face of the man who hit Travis Hensley.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Line nine,"</span> Ana says, without looking. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Did you see his face. No. I never saw his face. They asked. They wanted that one."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Contradicted. Line nine asks it straight out. She answers.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC06`, title:`Where the questions start`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana unzipped the freezer bag, the whole length of the seal, and turned the notebook to Consuelo's page, the one Gene started at five past nine. C. Baca, 64. And in the margin, in Gene's pencil, boy?`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He wrote that on a Sunday morning, sitting with her, and in December he couldn't tell you why."</span> She laid the pencil across the transcript at the second question, and Dani read it again under the lead. Were you awake at about a quarter past eleven on the night of Saturday, October 27, 2007? <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now look where the questions start. Then tell me what that gives you."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Whatever she saw or heard before a quarter past eleven, nobody asked her about it.`, s:3, best:true, say:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A quarter past eleven,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"First question after her address, and they put her at the sink at the moment they needed. Before that, the jury got nothing. If Gene's boy was anywhere, he was in the part nobody asked about."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. The prosecutor's first question about the night begins at a quarter past eleven, and nothing goes earlier, on direct or cross. So the transcript implies she was never asked about anything before then.`},
        {t:`The State didn't want the jury to hear about anything earlier that night in the lot.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Maybe,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's about what was in their heads. The page knows what they asked. It doesn't know why. Keep the why for when you can prove it."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. It fits the transcript, but it's a claim about motive, which the page can't support. The page supports what wasn't asked, not why.`},
        {t:`She told the jury she'd been standing at the sink since before the bar fight began.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She reads it again. At the sink, yes. At a quarter past eleven. Nobody asked how long she'd been there, and she never said.`,
          why:`Inference. Not in the transcript. She's asked where she was at a quarter past eleven, and nothing earlier.`},
        {t:`Phil asked her about the earlier part of that night, and she couldn't remember any of it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana" data-tone="Dry, flat, unhurried.">"Phil asked her one question,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana" data-tone="Dry, flat, unhurried.">"Who was it. She said no, sir. He sat down. That's all of Phil."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Contradicted. The cross is a single question about identification.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s5`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR10`, title:`Room 4`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She was early. She was always early. At 1:35 she parked on the far side of Highway 2, facing the vape shop that used to be the Night Owl, and turned off the engine and didn't get out.`,
        `The back lot was paved now, and striped. Six cars. A dumpster with a padlock. Across the street from it, small and yellow, with a chain link fence and a plaster Virgin by the steps, was the only house on the block.`,
        `Twenty five minutes. She opened the practice set on her phone, because that was what she did with twenty five minutes.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Practice set, question 9">Night nurse's log: The patient in Room 4 rang the call bell at 2:10, 2:40, and 3:15 A.M. Each time, the nurse found the window blind rattling in the draft from the heating vent and found nothing else wrong. Maintenance taped the blind down at 3:30. The patient did not ring again before the shift ended at 7:00 A.M.<br>The statements above, if true, most strongly support which one of the following?</prop>`,
        `Her mother had worked nights her whole life. Her mother would have taped the blind herself at 2:10 and not written it down.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Most likely, the rattling blind was at least part of why the patient rang.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Three rings, one rattle each time, nothing else wrong, and then the tape and no more rings. It doesn't prove the blind did it. It points there, and it points nowhere else. Most likely, at least part. The small words did the work.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. The pattern backs a modest causal claim: the blind was likely involved. "Most likely" and "at least part" keep the claim inside what the log shows.`},
        {t:`The patient rang the bell only over the blind, and for no other reason.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Only. She'd have picked this one in October. The log says the nurse found nothing else wrong. It doesn't say nothing else was wrong. Patients ring for reasons nurses never find.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Too strong. "Only" and "no other reason" go past what the log can show.`},
        {t:`Taping the blind down is what let the patient sleep through the rest of the night.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Close. But nobody wrote down sleep. The patient stopped ringing. A person can lie awake till seven without touching the bell. She'd added a fact to make the answer feel finished.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Partly backed, since the ringing stopped after the tape. But sleep is never mentioned. The answer adds a fact the log doesn't have.`},
        {t:`The heating vent in Room 4 was set way too high that night and needs to be repaired.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The vent made a draft. Nobody said it was set wrong, or broken. She'd fixed the building. The question was about the bell.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Not supported. The log mentions a draft from the vent, not that the vent was set wrong or needs repair.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s6`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR10`, title:`Smaller`, q:`What does Dani say to Consuelo about how big the man in red was?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Alma Baca had the door open before Dani got to it. Twenty seven. Hair scraped back. Red pen behind one ear, uncapped. She looked at Dani the way she probably looked at a kid sent down to the office for somebody else's fight. <span class="said" data-who="Alma">"She's eighty three. One hour. If she gets tired, you go."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Okay."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Alma" data-tone="Firm, flat, final.">"I mean it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I know you do."</span>`,
        `The kitchen smelled like cumin and the comal. Consuelo Baca was small and straight in a cardigan buttoned to the neck, and she didn't let Dani say one word until there was a bowl of albóndigas in front of her and a tortilla on top of the bowl to keep it warm. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Eat. Then."</span> Alma sat at the end of the table with a stack of spelling tests and didn't grade one of them.`,
        `Dani ate. Then she asked what she'd come to ask, and only that. What did you see.`,
        `Consuelo told it looking at the window over the sink, not at Dani. The sink, the earache, the man by the curb, the thing in his hand. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"The detective asked me how tall. I told him I couldn't say. I couldn't. There was nothing out there to measure him by."</span> She turned her hands over on the table. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Only the other one. The other one was a big man. Big in the shoulders. And this one was smaller than him. That I saw. Smaller."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"So he was smaller than the man he hit. That's what you could see."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Firm, short, final.">"Yes."</span> Consuelo nods once, hard, like a drawer shutting. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Firm, short, final.">"Smaller than the big one. That's all. Write that."</span> Dani writes SMALLER THAN THE BIG ONE. It fits her father. It fits half the men in Adams County. That's what it's worth, and she writes it at that size.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Her account backs one comparison, the attacker against the victim. It doesn't give a height and it doesn't rule anyone in or out. Dani keeps it exactly that size.`},
        {t:`"So he was a small man, then. Short. Not a big man like the other one."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Consuelo frowns. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I didn't say short. I said smaller. Next to that one, a lot of men were smaller."</span> She was right. Smaller than a big man isn't small.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Half right. He was smaller than the victim. "Short" turns a comparison into a size, and the account can't support that.`},
        {t:`"Was he about my dad's height? Pa's about five ten, I think."`, s:0, flag:`ledconsuelo`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Consuelo looks at her a long moment. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Maybe. I don't know your father, mija. I only saw him sitting down."</span> Then, slowly, trying it out: <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Slow, unsure, trying it out.">"Five ten. Maybe five ten."</span> Alma's pen stops. Dani hears the number come back to her in Consuelo's voice and knows exactly whose number it is.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Nothing in her account supports a height in feet and inches. And asked this way, it hands the witness the answer. Now "five ten" is in her mouth, and it came from Dani.`},
        {t:`"So he wasn't tall enough to have been my father, then. Was he, Mrs. Baca?"`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Plain, not unkind.">"How would I know that?"</span> Consuelo says, not unkindly. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I never saw your father stand next to anybody."</span> To rule him out, Dani would need his height and Travis's both, and she'd brought only what she wanted.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Not supported. To rule a man out by size you'd need his build and Travis's, and she gave neither. Smaller than V rules nobody out.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s7`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR10`, title:`Papi`, q:`What does Consuelo's memory of the boy most strongly support?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Light, offhand, unhurried.">"And before. Before the one in the jacket."</span> Consuelo said it almost in passing. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Light, offhand, unhurried.">"The little boy."</span>`,
        `Dani's pen stopped.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Soft, small, unsteady.">"Ten minutes to eleven, by the stove. Alma's drops were at eleven, every four hours, and I was watching the clock for them. Out there in the lot. Papi. Papi. Like that."</span> She said it softly, in a small voice, and it was worse than if she'd said it loud. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Soft, small, unsteady.">"Not screaming. Past screaming. You know how they get."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I went to the glass. At night, with the light on in here, all you see is yourself and the stove. Out there, the trucks, dark. Then she was crying for me too, so I went to her."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Sharp, indignant, remembering.">"I told the young policeman at the door. He wrote in his little book and he said, that's some kid from the apartments, ma'am. Some neighbor's kid. I said, what neighbor's kid is out at eleven o'clock in the back of a bar on a Saturday, crying for his papi? He said, yes ma'am."</span> She shrugged. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Flat, dry, resigned.">"The detective wrote it too, the next day. Then nobody asked me again. Not ever."</span>`,
        `Boy? In the margin. Nineteen years in a gun safe.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"So a little boy was out there in the lot that night, crying for his papi."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Yes. That."</span> Dani writes it down. BOY. PAPI. 10:50. She underlines 10:50 twice, and she doesn't write anything else.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. What she heard backs a child in the lot, in distress, calling "Papi." That's the claim at the size of the evidence. It names no one, and it doesn't say why he was crying.`},
        {t:`"So some kid saw the fight inside the bar, and it scared him half to death."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Inside? I don't know about inside."</span> Consuelo shakes her head. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"He was out there. Crying. That's what I know."</span> Dani had put the boy in a room nobody saw him in.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. A scared child is supported. That he saw the fight inside is an extra step the account doesn't take.`},
        {t:`"So it was a neighbor's kid, from the apartments, like the officer wrote it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Consuelo's mouth goes flat. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Flat, firm, curt.">"That's what he said. I'm telling you what I heard."</span> The officer's guess was never her account. Dani had just picked the officer's side over the witness's, in the witness's own kitchen.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Not supported. That's the officer's guess, not anything she heard, and she disputed it at the time.`},
        {t:`"So the boy saw the man in the jacket hit Travis. He was a witness too."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"No, mija. Before."</span> She taps the table. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Ten to eleven, the boy. The other was later."</span> Dani had folded two times into one.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. The timing contradicts it. She heard the boy at about 10:50. The attack came later.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s8`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR09`, title:`North`, q:`Taking that as true, which of these must also be true?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ten to eleven. Teo was at Abuela's. Everybody knew where Teo was.`,
        `Consuelo got up and took Dani's bowl to the sink, and stood there with it, looking out. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Come see."</span>`,
        `It was a small window. A geranium in a coffee can, a dish rack, the plaster Virgin's twin on the sill, faded to the color of milk. Through the glass, the paved lot. The new curb. A row of parking stripes. At the curb stood a light pole, and on the pole, zip tied at about shoulder height, were two purple Rockies pennants, one faded nearly pink and one under it still dark.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"His sister,"</span> Consuelo said, before Dani could ask. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Quiet, plain, tender.">"Every October. She never knocks. I leave the porch light on so she can see to do the ties."</span> Dani had never thought of anybody standing at that curb on purpose. She'd seen his sister once, across a courthouse hall in 2008, with her purse in both hands. Dani had been the one who looked away.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"The refinery men parked there in a row. Every Saturday. Trucks, trucks, trucks. And he ran that way."</span> She pointed with the bowl. North. Past the end of the stripes there was a chain link fence with a gap in it, and past the fence a ditch, a long gray trough of ice and dead cattails, and past the ditch the rail bed, raised up on its gravel, and the tracks. Past the tracks, a long way off, the refinery. Its lights never go off. In the daytime you can hardly see them. Nothing in between. No street. No driveway. Nowhere a truck could go.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"There's nothing back there. The ditch. The tracks. The kids go back there to drink. The young policeman and the other one walked it that night with their flashlights, all the way to the tracks. They didn't find anybody. Not a thing in his hand, not a thing on the ground."</span>`,
        `In January, on the basement floor, Teo had said on foot, and men with trucks drive, and she'd wondered what was north of that lot. She didn't need a map now. She put it the way Ana would, only what had to be true: the man ran north on foot, and north there's nowhere to drive.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`If that was Pa, then he ran on foot to a place his truck had no way to get to.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`If. That's all she gets to keep, and it's enough. The State's man ran away from a row of trucks into a ditch, and the State's man drove a truck. Nobody in 2008 had to explain that. North was a word in a report. Nobody stood at this sink and looked. She writes NORTH, NO ROAD and dates it.`,
          why:`Must be true. Whoever ran north went where no truck could go. So if the man was Luis, he went where his truck couldn't follow. That's guaranteed by the facts, and it's conditional. It doesn't claim the man wasn't Luis.`},
        {t:`The man who ran away from the curb didn't have a truck anywhere that night.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's what Teo wanted, sitting on the basement floor. It might even be true. But a man can leave a truck on another street and walk back to it the long way.`,
          why:`Must be true. Could be true, and the view makes it more likely. But the facts don't rule out a vehicle parked somewhere else.`},
        {t:`The man who ran from the curb wasn't Pa, because Pa's truck was parked right there.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She wants it so badly she has to say it twice in her head to hear it. A scared man runs. Truck or no truck. The window makes it harder for the man to be Pa. It doesn't make it impossible.`,
          why:`Must be true. Too strong. Running away from a vehicle is strange, not impossible. Nothing here guarantees the man wasn't Luis.`},
        {t:`The man ran back there to hide whatever he'd used, and then circled around.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Nobody saw that. Consuelo saw him run. Whatever he did in the dark past the fence, nobody wrote it down, because nobody knew.`,
          why:`Must be true. Not supported at all. Nothing in the account says what he did after he ran.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR10`, title:`The stove clock`, q:`What does the stove clock tell Dani about the crying?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Back at the table. The clock, Dani said. Just that. What time, and how she knew it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"That one."</span> The stove: white enamel gone yellow at the knobs, a clock set in the back panel, its second hand still going round. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Quiet, careful, exact.">"Ten to eleven, the boy. I was watching it for the drops. Eleven, drops. Then I sat on her bed and sang her the whole song. Twice. Three times. Till she quit."</span> She smiled down the table at Alma, who was reading one spelling test very hard. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Then up to rinse the dropper. That's when. At the sink."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Did you hear the boy again?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Quiet, certain, heavy.">"No. Not after. I listened. I sang and I listened."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"So whatever made him cry happened before eleven, before the man in red."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Yes,"</span> Consuelo says. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Before. A long time before. Three songs' worth."</span> Dani draws it like Ana would, a line and two ticks. 10:50, boy. 11:15, jacket. Two things on one night, and half an hour between them that nobody ever wrote down.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Boy at about 10:50. Attack after the eleven o'clock drops. So whatever set him off came first. That much the clock gives you. Not why.`},
        {t:`"So by the time the man in the jacket came out to the curb, the boy was long gone."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I didn't hear him. That's what I know."</span> A shrug. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Maybe he stopped. Maybe somebody took him. I didn't hear."</span>`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Partly. She didn't hear him again, fine. Kids go quiet without going anywhere. Long gone is more than she gave.`},
        {t:`"So the boy was out there crying right when the man in the jacket hit Travis."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Firm, short, a little impatient.">"No."</span> Firm. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Firm, short, a little impatient.">"I told you. Before. I was singing."</span> She'd just said so, and Dani had heard the two sounds as one night instead of two times.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Contradicted. The crying came at about 10:50 and stopped. The attack came after eleven.`},
        {t:`"So he was crying because of the fight inside, at twenty five after ten."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Consuelo doesn't know about twenty five after ten. She never saw inside. Dani had borrowed Ronnie's clock to explain a sound Ronnie never heard.`,
          why:`Most strongly supported. Not supported. Nothing in her account ties the crying to the fight in the bar, and she has no knowledge of it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s10`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC04`, title:`My words`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ten to three. Alma had looked at the stove clock twice, and the third time she said <span class="said" data-who="Alma">"Abuela"</span> in the voice you'd use on a third grader stalling at the pencil sharpener. Consuelo waved her off. Dani was putting the cap back on her pen, and before she could close the notebook Consuelo leaned over and set a hand on it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"You were the girl,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"At the courthouse. In the hall, in that blue dress. Somebody should have hemmed those sleeves."</span> Dani said she remembered the dress. Her mother had bought it the night before, on sale, a size up so it would last. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I read what you said in the paper,"</span> Consuelo said. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"The jacket and the washer."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Quiet, bitter, steady.">"All I said was red. I said it to the police and I said it to the jury, and then the man in the tie got up and said it ten more times, and every time he said it he got closer to your father's name."</span> She looked straight at Dani for the first time since the door. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Quiet, direct, heavy.">"They used my words too, mija."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"You told them what you saw. They're the ones who said whose jacket it was."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"Yes,"</span> Consuelo says, and holds on to Dani's wrist for a second. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Firm, fierce, sure.">"That's right. I said red. I never said a name, not one time, and I'd swear to that in front of anybody."</span> At the far end of the table Alma pushes her chair back, goes to the sink, and stands there running the tap with her back to them.`,
          why:`Would agree with. She says it: "I said red," and the name came from someone else. Dani's reply puts the line where Consuelo puts it. What she saw, her side. Who it was, theirs.`},
        {t:`"So you think they convicted the wrong man. You've thought so all this time."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo">"I didn't say that."</span> The hand comes off the notebook. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Quiet, ashamed, honest.">"I don't know. That's the whole sin, mija. I don't know, and they let me sound like I knew."</span>`,
          why:`Would agree with. Half. Stretched words, yes, she'd sign that. Wrong man? She never says so, and she says flat out she doesn't know.`},
        {t:`"You should have told them you weren't sure the jacket was really red."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Flat, hard, proud.">"It was red."</span> Flat. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Flat, hard, proud.">"I know red. Don't take that from me too."</span> She was sure of the one thing. That was never the trouble.`,
          why:`Would agree with. Contradicted. She has never doubted the color. Her complaint is about what was done with it.`},
        {t:`"None of it was your fault, Mrs. Baca. You have to know that. Not any of it."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Dry, wry, unyielding.">"Don't tell an old woman what isn't her fault."</span> A little smile, no give in it. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Dry, wry, unyielding.">"I go to confession for less than this."</span> Kind, and not something she'd ever say.`,
          why:`Would agree with. Not her view. She carries it as hers. A comforting line isn't the same as a claim she'd agree with.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR09`, title:`Half an hour`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At the door Consuelo put a hand on each side of Dani's face, the way you'd check a child for fever. <span class="said" data-who="Consuelo" data-tone="Soft, tender, sure.">"Yours too, mija. They used yours too."</span> Dani thanked her. She'd meant it when she said it to Consuelo. Coming back the other way, she couldn't take it.`,
        `Alma walked her out. On the porch she pulled the door shut behind them, almost all the way, and spoke low.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Alma" data-tone="Low, quiet, protective.">"She went to confession every Saturday for a year after that trial. Every Saturday. She hadn't done anything."</span> She looked across the street at the lot. <span class="said" data-who="Alma" data-tone="Low, firm, protective.">"Don't bring her into this for nothing."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"It isn't for nothing."</span> Alma held her eyes a second longer than was comfortable. <span class="said" data-who="Alma">"I remember the song,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Alma">"Nothing else. I've tried. For her, I've tried."</span> She went back in.`,
        `In the car Dani left the engine running for the heat and called Ana. She read her the notebook page, her own, word for word. Smaller. Papi. Ten to eleven. North, and nothing back there. When she got to Papi, Ana didn't say anything for long enough that Dani heard Dicta's tags rattle somewhere behind her.`,
        ...((s.flags.ledconsuelo||G.ledconsuelo)
          ?[`When she got to five ten she stopped. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"That one's mine,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Dani" data-tone="Quiet, flat, owning it.">"I asked it wrong. She gave it back to me."</span> The line was quiet. <span class="said" data-who="Ana" data-tone="Quiet, firm, kind.">"Then write it down as yours,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana" data-tone="Quiet, firm, kind.">"Next to hers. Don't you dare leave it out."</span>`]
          :[]),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana" data-tone="Quiet, slow, approving.">"Her words,"</span> Ana said finally. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Write all of it out tonight. Hers. Not yours."</span> Then, briskly: <span class="said" data-who="Ana" data-tone="Brisk, quick, businesslike.">"Now the record. What the jury had. Ronnie, out the back at a quarter to eleven. Consuelo, a man in red at the curb at a quarter past. The State, that man was your father. Take all three as true. What has to be?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That Pa was near the lot, or back at it, half an hour after he left the bar."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Line four,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Near the lot at 11:15. Nobody ever proved it. They didn't have to. If all three are true it just is, and the jury filled in the half hour by itself."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. If he went out the back at 10:45 and he was the man at the curb at 11:15, then at 11:15 he was there, whether he stayed or returned. Nothing more is needed, and nothing more follows.`},
        {t:`"That Pa lied to Gene when he said he drove home alone at a quarter to eleven."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Wrong, maybe,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He could've driven home and come back. And lied is a word about what's in a man's head. These three give you where he was. Not what he meant."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. Close. If the State is right, his account can't be the whole story. But he could have left at 10:45 and returned, and "lied" claims intent the facts can't prove.`},
        {t:`"That Pa went back inside the bar sometime after Ronnie told him to leave."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Inside?"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The curb's outside. Nobody needs him back through that door for any of this. Could be. Doesn't have to be."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. Could be true. None of the three statements requires him to go back inside.`},
        {t:`"That Pa was the only man in a red jacket anywhere near that lot that night."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That would've proved it for them,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It isn't what follows from it. Twenty men in red, and one of them still could be him. You've got it backward."</span>`,
          why:`Must be true. Backward. "Only man in red" is a premise that would make the State's conclusion follow. Granting the conclusion doesn't make that premise true.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR11`, title:`Can't be`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now his,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Say your father's right. He was in the truck and gone before eleven, and he didn't come back that night. And say Consuelo's right about a quarter past. Put them side by side. Which of these can't be true?"</span>`,
        `Over at the house the front curtain moved. Alma, checking. It didn't close all the way.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That Pa was the man who hit Travis at the curb and ran north on foot."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the one,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And it's why nobody in 2008 ever put a minute on when he drove away. Pick any minute before 11:15 and they'd need him coming back. They never had to say it, so they never did."</span>`,
          why:`Must be false. If he was gone before eleven and never came back, he wasn't at the curb at a quarter past. So he can't be the man Consuelo saw. Take both accounts as true, and it's the one claim that can't be.`},
        {t:`"That Pa had a ball cap on his head at some point that night, even for a minute."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Probably false,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Ronnie says bareheaded, you say never. But that's Ronnie and you. These two accounts don't say a word about a cap on him. Can't be is a different animal."</span>`,
          why:`Must be false. Likely false from other evidence, but nothing in the two accounts given rules it out. Don't import facts into a cannot be true question.`},
        {t:`"That Pa threw a punch at Travis inside the bar at twenty five after ten."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana doesn't say anything. She doesn't have to. Ten twenty five is before both of them. A man can throw a punch at ten twenty five and be gone by eleven. Dani picked the one she was most afraid was true.`,
          why:`Must be false. Could be true. Nothing in either account touches the fight inside, which comes before both.`},
        {t:`"That the man who ran wore the same red jacket all the refinery men wore."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Could be,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Could be that's the whole point. A jacket like his on a man who isn't him. That one's not impossible. It's our case."</span>`,
          why:`Must be false. Could be true. A matching jacket on another man fits both accounts.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f5s4`, title:`Must be true, could be true, or must be false, then the arrows`, intro:`Ana's index cards. Cards one to seven give some facts and one statement. Take the facts as true. Do they prove the statement, leave it open, or rule it out? Cards eight to twenty are the arrows from The whole table, easy first: deny a sentence plainly, pick the arrow a rule makes, or say what a rule lets you conclude.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. Facts: Every room on the third floor of the Hale Building has a window. Room 312 is on the third floor. Statement: Room 312 has a window.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:0, why:`Every third floor room has one, and 312 is a third floor room. The facts prove it.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. Facts: Most of the singers in the Linden choir sing tenor. Lena sings in the Linden choir. Statement: Lena sings tenor.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:1, why:`Most isn't all. Lena could be one of the tenors or one of the others.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. Facts: No car in Lot B belongs to anyone who works the night desk. Marco works the night desk. Statement: Marco's car is parked in Lot B.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:2, why:`If it were Marco's car in Lot B, a night desk worker would own a car there. The facts rule that out.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. Facts: Every bus on Route 6 stops at the library. The Number 14 bus stops at the library. Statement: The Number 14 is a Route 6 bus.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:1, why:`Route 6 buses stop at the library, but so might buses on other routes. The rule runs one way only.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. Facts: On Tuesday the bakery baked one batch of rye and no more all day, and the batch sold out by nine. Jo got there at ten. Statement: Jo bought a loaf of rye at the bakery when she got there Tuesday.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:2, why:`None left after nine, none baked after. A ten o'clock loaf is off the table.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. Facts: Some of the jurors were teachers. Every teacher on the jury lived in Brighton. Statement: Some of the jurors lived in Brighton.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:0, why:`Those teachers were jurors. Every one of them lived in Brighton. So some jurors did. Done.`},
      {prompt:`Card seven. Facts: Five people saw the car. Three said it was green. Two said it was blue. Statement: The car was green.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:1, why:`Careful. Five people talking about a car is a fact about five people. Green won the vote. The car doesn't vote.`},
      {prompt:`Card eight. Deny it plainly: All of the tenants signed the lease.`, choices:[`None of the tenants signed the lease.`,`At least one tenant didn't sign the lease.`,`Most of the tenants didn't sign the lease.`], answer:1, why:`All goes to some not. One holdout is enough to make the sentence false, so that's all the denial says. None and most go much further than you need.`, task:`LR04`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card nine. Deny it plainly: Some of the boxes in the hall were opened.`, choices:[`Some of the boxes in the hall weren't opened.`,`Not all of the boxes in the hall were opened.`,`None of the boxes in the hall were opened.`], answer:2, why:`Some goes to none. Some opened and some unopened can both be true at once, so the first two don't deny anything.`, task:`LR04`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card ten. Deny it plainly: Most of the votes were counted by noon.`, choices:[`Most of the votes weren't counted by noon.`,`Half or fewer of the votes were counted by noon.`,`None of the votes were counted by noon.`], answer:1, why:`Most means more than half, so the denial is half or fewer. Exactly half counted makes the sentence false and makes the first choice false too. None is the far swing.`, task:`LR04`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card eleven. Deny it: The clerk was both early and polite.`, choices:[`The clerk wasn't early, or wasn't polite, or both.`,`The clerk was late, or rude, or both of those.`,`The clerk was neither early nor polite.`], answer:0, why:`Deny an and and you get an or of the two denials. One failure is enough. Late and rude are opposites, not denials: a clerk who's right on time and polite makes the sentence false without being late or rude. Neither demands both failures.`, task:`LR04`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card twelve. Deny it: The van was either at the shop or in the lot.`, choices:[`It wasn't at the shop, or it wasn't in the lot.`,`It was at the shop and in the lot both.`,`It was neither at the shop nor in the lot.`], answer:2, why:`Deny an or and you get an and. For the or to be false, both places have to come up empty. The first choice is what you'd write to deny an and.`, task:`LR04`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card thirteen. Rule: You may check out a laptop only if you've signed the form. Which arrow does it make?`, choices:[`Signed the form, then you may check out a laptop.`,`Checked out a laptop, then you signed the form.`,`Didn't check one out, then you didn't sign the form.`], answer:1, why:`Only if puts what follows it on the back of the arrow. Checking out needs the form. Signing it doesn't promise you a laptop, and the third choice denies both sides of the backward rule.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card fourteen. Rule: The ferry doesn't run unless the wind is under thirty knots. Which follows?`, choices:[`If the wind is under thirty knots, the ferry runs.`,`If the ferry isn't running, the wind is thirty knots or more.`,`If the ferry is running, the wind is under thirty knots.`], answer:2, why:`Unless means if not. Wind not under thirty, then no ferry. Flip it: ferry running, so the wind's under thirty. Calm wind doesn't promise a ferry, and a docked ferry could be down for repairs.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card fifteen. Rule: No one may join the choir without an audition. Mara joined the choir. Statement: Mara auditioned.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:0, why:`No A without B is A, then B. Joined, so auditioned.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card sixteen. Rule: No one may join the choir without an audition. Theo auditioned. Statement: Theo joined the choir.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:1, why:`An audition is needed, not enough. Theo may have sung flat. The rule runs one way.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card seventeen. Rule: If a plant gets full sun and regular water, it flowers. This plant didn't flower. Which follows?`, choices:[`It got regular water but not full sun.`,`It didn't get full sun, and it didn't get regular water.`,`It didn't get full sun, or it didn't get regular water.`], answer:2, why:`Flip a rule with an and in front, and the and turns to or. At least one of the two was missing. You don't get to say which one, or that both were.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card eighteen. Rule: Anyone who runs the meeting either chairs the board or was named by the chair. Pat ran the meeting and doesn't chair the board. Statement: Pat was named by the chair.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:0, why:`Ran it, so one of the two. Not the first, so the second. That's an or on the back of the arrow, and the facts close one door.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card nineteen. Deny it: If the lab is open, a technician is on duty.`, choices:[`The lab is open and no technician is on duty.`,`The lab is closed and no technician is on duty.`,`If the lab is closed, no technician is on duty.`], answer:0, why:`A rule is false only when the front happens and the back doesn't. An open lab with nobody on duty breaks it. A closed lab tells the rule nothing.`, task:`LR04`, tags:[`COND`]},
      {prompt:`Card twenty. Rule: No dog without a rabies tag is boarded at the kennel unless its owner shows a vet's letter. Biscuit was boarded and has no tag. Statement: Biscuit's owner showed a vet's letter.`, choices:[`Must be true`,`Could be true`,`Must be false`], answer:0, why:`No tag and no letter, then not boarded. Flip it: boarded, so a tag or a letter. Biscuit has no tag, so the letter it is.`, task:`LR09`, tags:[`COND`]}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Two accounts of what Consuelo Baca saw: the detective's report of her statement, October 28, 2007, and Dani's notes, February 13, 2027`,
      text:[`Passage A`,
        `Witness statement of Consuelo Baca, as typed into Detective G. Vigil's report from his interview notes. The witness, 64, resides across the street from the rear lot of the Night Owl Tap. Interviewed at her residence on Sunday, October 28, 2007, at 0905 hours. On Saturday, October 27, 2007, at approximately 2315 hours, the witness was awake caring for her granddaughter, age 8, who had an earache. From her kitchen window the witness observed two males near the curb at the rear of the lot. The witness states that a male wearing a red jacket struck the victim with an object held in his hand. The victim fell backward and did not get up. The male in the red jacket fled north on foot toward the railroad tracks. The witness did not see the male's face and could not estimate his height. The witness telephoned 911 immediately. The witness was unable to provide any further description of the suspect.`,
        `Passage B`,
        `Dani's notes, Saturday, February 13, 2027, at Mrs. Baca's kitchen table, in her words. The stove clock said ten to eleven. I know because Alma's drops were at eleven, every four hours, so I was watching it. Then a little boy, out in the lot. Papi, Papi. Not screaming. Past that. I went to the glass, but you know how it is with the light on in here. All you see is you. Then she cried for me, so I went. Drops at eleven. I sang to her, three times through, till she quit. I was listening the whole time. That boy, I never heard him again.`,
        `Then I got up to rinse the dropper. That's when. Two men by the curb, under the streetlight, the only light back there. One big. Big in the shoulders. The other one smaller than the big one. That's all. Write that. A height, no. What do I measure him by? The other man. Red jacket. A cap. Something in his hand. The big one goes down backward. The other one runs that way, north, where there's nothing. The ditch. The tracks.`,
        `The young policeman wrote down the boy. Some neighbor's kid, he says. I said to him, what neighbor's kid is out at eleven o'clock in the back of a bar on a Saturday, crying for his papi? The detective wrote it too. Then nobody asked me again. At the trial they asked what was he wearing. I said red. The cap, nobody asked. The boy, nobody asked. Nothing else. I said red one time, and the man in the tie said it ten more times. A name, I never said. They used my words too.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: the wine dark sea`,
      text:[`In 1858 William Gladstone, then a member of Parliament and later Britain's prime minister, published a long study of Homer, and one chapter of it puzzled readers more than the rest. Gladstone had gone through the Iliad and the Odyssey counting color words. Homer calls the sea wine dark, and gives the same word to oxen. He uses one word for honey and for fresh green twigs. Blue, as a word of its own, hardly appears. From these counts Gladstone concluded that the Greeks of Homer's day perceived color less finely than modern Europeans, and that the human sense of color had been developing ever since.`,
        `The idea traveled. A German philologist found similar gaps in ancient Hebrew, Sanskrit, and Icelandic writing and argued that languages acquire their color words in a regular order, with blue among the last. An eye doctor went further and proposed that the retina itself had changed over a few thousand years. The pattern in the old texts was real. The trouble lay in the step from the words on the page to the eyes of the people who wrote them.`,
        `Nobody could test the eyes of an ancient Greek, but researchers could test living speakers of languages with few color words. In the late 1960s two American researchers, Brent Berlin and Paul Kay, asked speakers of many languages to name and sort color chips. The chips were standard color samples, so every speaker was looking at exactly the same thing. They reported that languages differ widely in how many basic color words they have, and that when a language has only a few, those words tend to arrive in a similar order: words for dark and light first, then red, and blue usually later. Yet speakers whose languages use one word for both blue and green can still tell a blue chip from a green one when they see them side by side. A missing word is not a missing color.`,
        `Later work has disputed parts of that sequence and the way its languages were sampled. The larger correction has held up. Homer's wine dark may describe the sea's sheen, or its mood at dusk, rather than its hue, since a word can carry a texture or a comparison as easily as a wavelength. What a text leaves unnamed shows what its writers found worth naming, and perhaps what their language made easy to say. It does not show what they could see.`,
        `Gladstone was not careless. He counted with great patience, and his counts are still cited. Nobody needs to doubt his arithmetic to doubt his conclusion. His mistake came after the counting, when a fact about vocabulary was read as a fact about vision. The words were evidence, and good evidence. They were evidence about language first.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Every volunteer at the Ash Street food bank who works the Saturday shift has completed the safety training. Anyone who has completed the safety training is required to attend the monthly volunteers' meeting. Rosa Delgado has volunteered at the food bank for two years and works the Saturday shift.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,
      opts:[`Every volunteer who attends the monthly meeting works the Saturday shift.`,`Rosa Delgado has attended every monthly meeting in the past two years.`,`Rosa Delgado is required to attend the monthly volunteers' meeting.`,`Only Saturday shift volunteers have completed the safety training.`,`Some volunteers who skip the monthly meeting work the Saturday shift.`],
      a:2, why:`Must be true. Join the two rules: Saturday shift means training, and training means the meeting is required. Rosa works Saturdays, so the meeting is required of her. The meeting rule doesn't say everyone there works Saturdays, and the training rule runs one way, so other volunteers may have trained too. Being required isn't the same as having attended every one. And required isn't the same as went: a Saturday volunteer could still skip, so the last choice could be true, but nothing proves it.`, task:`LR09`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Club secretary: More than half of our club's forty members played in the spring tournament, and more than half of our members also belong to the library's Thursday reading group. Every member who played in the tournament paid the entry fee out of their own pocket.<br><br>If the secretary's statements are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,
      opts:[`At least one member of the reading group paid a tournament entry fee.`,`Most members of the reading group played in the spring tournament.`,`More of the club's members played in the tournament than read with the group.`,`Some members who paid an entry fee don't belong to the reading group.`,`No member of the club who skipped the tournament belongs to the reading group.`],
      a:0, why:`Must be true. Two groups that each take more than half of the same forty people have to share at least one member. That member played, so that member paid. Most of the readers playing, which group is bigger, whether some payers aren't readers, and the last choice are all left open.`, task:`LR09`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`The first ferry of the day leaves Port Ellis at 6:00 A.M., and ferries leave every forty minutes after that until the last one at 9:20 P.M. The crossing takes at least thirty five minutes. On Tuesday, Ines Carvalho took a ferry from Port Ellis and arrived on the far shore before 7:00 A.M.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,
      opts:[`Ines arrived on the far shore at exactly 6:35 A.M.`,`The 6:40 ferry reached the far shore before 7:15 A.M.`,`Ines returned to Port Ellis on Tuesday's last ferry.`,`Ines was the first passenger to board that morning.`,`Ines took the first ferry that left Port Ellis Tuesday.`],
      a:4, why:`Must be true. The second ferry leaves at 6:40 and takes at least thirty five minutes, so it can't arrive before 7:15. Only the 6:00 ferry gets her there before seven. "At least" means the arrival needn't be exactly 6:35, and it rules out the 6:40 arriving before 7:15. Nothing says how she returned or who boarded first.`, task:`LR09`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`In the six years since the Holt River dam was removed, the number of salmon counted spawning upstream of the old dam site has risen every year. Over the same six years, salmon counts in the region's other rivers, none of which had a dam removed, have stayed about the same.<br><br>The information above most strongly supports which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`Removing a dam from any river will increase the number of salmon that spawn in it.`,`The dam was the only thing that had kept salmon from spawning upstream on the Holt.`,`The salmon spawning upstream on the Holt came from the region's other rivers.`,`Something about the dam's removal likely helped raise the salmon count upstream.`,`Salmon counts in the region's other rivers will start to fall in the next few years.`],
      a:3, why:`Most strongly supported. The rise on the Holt alone, while the comparison rivers held steady, points to the one thing that changed there. "Likely helped" is the right size. Any river, the only thing, and where the fish came from all go further than the counts. Nothing supports a prediction about the other rivers.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`A survey of the Orchard Street neighborhood found that households with at least one member who works night shifts are far more likely than other households to own blackout curtains. Even so, only a small share of all the blackout curtains in the neighborhood are in households with a night shift worker.<br><br>The survey's findings most strongly support which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`Most people who work night shifts don't own blackout curtains at all.`,`Households in the neighborhood without a night shift worker don't use curtains.`,`Most blackout curtains in the neighborhood were bought before the survey.`,`Blackout curtains are not much use to people who work night shifts.`,`Households with a night shift worker are a small part of the neighborhood.`],
      a:4, why:`Most strongly supported. If night shift households are much likelier to own the curtains and still hold only a small share of them, the likeliest reason is that there aren't many such households. The first choice is about people, not households, and more likely than others doesn't tell you whether most own them. The others say things the survey never measured.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`]},
    {q:`At Westover General Hospital, no one enters the neonatal unit on any day without being screened at the front desk that day. Everyone screened at the front desk is given a badge dated that day. No one holding a badge dated that day is admitted to the surgical wing unless a nurse escorts them. On Tuesday, Ilse Varga entered the neonatal unit.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following must also be true?`,
      opts:[`Ilse Varga was admitted to the surgical wing on Tuesday with a nurse escorting her.`,`Everyone screened at the front desk on Tuesday went on to enter the neonatal unit.`,`Anyone a nurse escorted into the surgical wing on Tuesday had been in the neonatal unit.`,`If Ilse Varga was admitted to the surgical wing on Tuesday, a nurse escorted her there.`,`No one without a badge dated Tuesday was admitted to the surgical wing on Tuesday.`],
      a:3, why:`Must be true, a chain. Ilse entered the unit, so she was screened that day, so she holds a badge dated that day, so the surgical wing takes her only with a nurse. Nothing says she went to the surgical wing at all, so the first choice could be false. Everyone screened entering the unit, and everyone escorted having been in it, turn arrows around. No badge, no admission denies both sides of the last rule.`, task:`LR09`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Survey director: Of the Harlow County residents we surveyed, most said they oppose the proposed highway. And most of the residents who said they oppose the highway also said they plan to vote in the spring election. It follows that most of the residents we surveyed plan to vote in the spring election.<br><br>The survey director's reasoning is flawed because it`,
      opts:[`treats a majority of one smaller group as if it were a majority of everyone surveyed`,`takes for granted that residents who say they plan to vote will in fact go and vote`,`assumes that every resident who opposes the highway opposes it for the same reason`,`relies on a survey of residents who may not represent the county as a whole`,`concludes that the highway will be defeated on the strength of opinion alone`],
      a:0, why:`Flaw, counting words. Most of those surveyed oppose the highway, and most of the opponents plan to vote. The second most counts only the opponents, so the planned voters could be just over a quarter of everyone surveyed. The conclusion is about plans and about the people surveyed, so whether plans are kept and whether the sample fits the county don't touch the step. Reasons for opposing and the highway's fate never come up.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Every bus that stops at Garner Station also stops at Linden Square. No bus that stops at Linden Square runs after midnight. The Route 9 bus runs until 1:00 A.M. every night. The Route 4 bus stops at Linden Square but not at Garner Station.<br><br>If the statements above are true, each of the following could be true EXCEPT:`,
      opts:[`The Route 4 bus stops running before midnight every night.`,`The Route 9 bus stops at neither of the two places named.`,`The Route 9 bus runs during the evening as well as after midnight.`,`Some buses that stop at Linden Square stop at Garner Station.`,`The Route 9 bus stops at Garner Station on some of its runs.`],
      a:4, why:`Must be false. A bus that stops at Garner stops at Linden Square, and a Linden Square bus doesn't run after midnight. Route 9 runs after midnight, so it can't stop at Linden Square, and so it can't stop at Garner. Route 4 can't run after midnight, so stopping before midnight fits. Route 9 skipping both, Garner buses stopping at Linden Square, and Route 9 running in the evening all fit too.`, task:`LR11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Science teacher: To win the regional science fair, a project must include data the student collected personally. Mai Tran's project on soil bacteria includes data she collected herself, every weekend for six months. So Mai's project will win the regional fair.<br><br>The teacher's reasoning is flawed because it`,
      opts:[`treats something a winner must have as enough on its own to win`,`assumes that six months is long enough to study soil bacteria`,`relies on Mai's own account of how she collected her data`,`presumes that the regional fair has only one winning project`,`fails to say how other students' projects were judged before`],
      a:0, why:`Flaw. Original data is required to win, which makes it necessary, not sufficient. Lots of projects with their own data can lose. The teacher never claims six months is enough, never relies on Mai's account, and the number of winners and past judging don't touch the step.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately describes the relationship between Passage A and Passage B?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`Passage B withdraws the description the witness gave in Passage A and offers a new one in its place.`,`Passage B gives the witness's own account of the events in Passage A, with details Passage A leaves out.`,`Passage A summarizes Passage B, leaving out the details that the detective considered unimportant.`,`Passage B disputes the events reported in Passage A and argues that a different man struck the victim.`,`Passage A reports what the witness saw, and Passage B reports what her neighbors said about it later.`],
      a:1, why:`Comparative passages. Both tell the same night: the curb, the red jacket, the blow, north on foot, no height. B is in her own voice and adds what A leaves out, the cap, the boy and the stove clock, and it ends with what the trial did with her words. Nothing is withdrawn, A was written first, B names no other man, and B is her account, not the neighbors'.`, task:`RC14`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following is stated in both passages?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`The man in the red jacket ran north on foot after the other man fell.`,`The witness heard the boy at ten minutes to eleven by her stove clock.`,`The witness was asked at trial about nothing but the man's clothing.`,`The witness could see the attacker's face clearly under the streetlight.`,`The witness gave her granddaughter medicine at eleven that night.`],
      a:0, why:`Comparative passages, what both say. A: he fled north on foot toward the railroad tracks. B: the other one runs that way, north, where there's nothing, the ditch and the tracks. The stove clock, the trial questions and the eleven o'clock drops are only in B. Neither says she saw a face, and A says she didn't.`, task:`RC14`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`According to Passage B, the witness knew what time she heard the boy because`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`the detective told her the time when he took her statement the next morning`,`she called 911 a few minutes later, and the call was logged with the time`,`the boy stopped crying at the moment the bar shut its doors for the night`,`she was watching the stove clock for her granddaughter's eleven o'clock drops`,`the young policeman at her door wrote the time down in his small notebook`],
      a:3, why:`Detail. B says the stove clock said ten to eleven and the drops were due at eleven, so she was watching it. Her call came after the attack, not the crying. Nothing says the detective gave her a time, the bar closed, or the officer wrote a time down.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`In Passage B, the word "smaller" is used to mean`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`short, compared with most of the grown men in the neighborhood`,`less large than the man who was struck, her only measure`,`younger than the man who was struck, judging by how he ran`,`farther from her window than the other man was standing`,`less important to her that night than the boy she'd heard`],
      a:1, why:`Meaning in context. The big one was big in the shoulders, the other was smaller than him, and her only measure for him was the other man. Smaller is a comparison with the man who fell, not a height. Short for a grown man is the reading the passage rules out. Age, distance and importance aren't what the word is doing.`, task:`RC07`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Based on the two passages, the witness would most likely agree with which one of the following?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`The detective should have written down a height for the man in 2007.`,`Her account was mistaken about the cap, as the trial later showed.`,`What she reported was used at trial to support a claim she never made.`,`The boy she heard was most likely a child from the nearby apartments.`,`A witness who is sure of the clothing should be willing to give a name.`],
      a:2, why:`Agree. B ends with it: she said red, the man in the tie said it ten more times, she never said a name, and they used her words too. What she saw became an identification she never gave. She says nobody could get a height from her, she repeats the cap in B, the neighbor's kid is the young policeman's guess, and she's proud of never naming anyone.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Which one of the following can be most reasonably inferred from the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Gladstone counted Homer's color words carelessly and got many of them wrong.`,`Gladstone's counts could be accurate though his conclusion about vision was not.`,`Homer used the word wine dark for the sea only when describing it at dusk.`,`Berlin and Kay found that most languages have a separate word for blue.`,`The retina of the human eye has changed little since Homer's lifetime.`],
      a:1, why:`Inference. The author says Gladstone counted with great patience and his counts are still cited, and that his mistake came after the counting. So the counts can stand while the conclusion falls. Careless is contradicted. Dusk is offered as one possible meaning, not a rule. Nothing says most languages have a word for blue, and the passage never measures the retina.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The passage suggests that the research described in the third paragraph could do which one of the following that the nineteenth century debate could not?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`prove that Homer could see the color blue as clearly as people today`,`show that every language adds its color words in exactly the same order`,`check whether a missing color word goes with an inability to see that color`,`find the original meaning of the Greek word that is translated as wine dark`,`explain why blue appears so rarely in ancient Hebrew and Icelandic writing`],
      a:2, why:`Inference. The paragraph opens by noting that nobody could test an ancient Greek's eyes but researchers could test living speakers, and it reports that people with one word for blue and green could still tell them apart. That's a direct check of whether a missing word means a missing color. It proves nothing about Homer himself, the order was only a tendency, and the passage doesn't recover the word's meaning or explain the old texts.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`The author would be most likely to agree with which one of the following statements?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Nineteenth century scholars should not have studied color words in old texts.`,`The pattern of color words in Homer's poems was invented by later scholars.`,`Berlin and Kay's sequence has been confirmed in every detail by later work.`,`A writer's choice of words says more about language than about eyesight.`,`Homer's wine dark must have meant the sea's mood rather than its color.`],
      a:3, why:`Would agree with. The last paragraph says the words "were evidence about language first," and the fourth says what a text leaves unnamed "does not show what they could see." The author praises the counting, calls the pattern real, notes later work disputed the sequence, and offers the sea's mood as one possibility with "may."`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Based on the passage, Gladstone would most likely have agreed with which one of the following?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`A word for blue can be missing from a language whose speakers see blue clearly.`,`The words a poet uses for color reveal how finely the poet's people saw color.`,`Homer used wine dark to describe the sheen of the sea rather than its hue.`,`Color words in ancient texts tell us more about language than about vision.`,`Speakers of every language can sort color chips into the same basic groups.`],
      a:1, why:`Would agree with. Gladstone reasoned from Homer's color words to a claim that the Greeks perceived color less finely. The first and fourth choices are the later correction he didn't make. The sheen reading is a modern suggestion, and the color chips come from research a century after him.`, task:`RC04`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`The author mentions the eye doctor's proposal that the retina itself had changed primarily in order to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`give evidence that the human retina has changed since ancient times`,`explain why Berlin and Kay chose to test living speakers with color chips`,`show how far others carried the conclusion Gladstone drew from his counts`,`suggest that medical evidence later confirmed the German philologist's order`,`concede that Gladstone's counting of Homer's color words contained errors`],
      a:2, why:`Function of a detail. The eye doctor "went further," the last step in a paragraph about how the idea traveled. The author doesn't endorse the retina claim; the point of the passage is that the step from words to eyes failed. Berlin and Kay's reasons aren't tied to the eye doctor. Nothing medical confirms the philologist. And the author says Gladstone counted with great patience.`, task:`RC08`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`In a study of 1,200 adults, researchers compared people who read printed books for at least thirty minutes a day with people who read the same amount but only on screens. Over six years, the print readers showed slightly less decline on memory tests. But the print readers were also older on average at the start, and in the general population older adults decline faster on such tests than younger adults do. Among participants who read neither print nor screens, decline matched what researchers expected for their age. The researchers didn't record how participants spent the rest of their time.<br><br>If the statements above are true, which one of the following is most strongly supported by them?`,
      opts:[`Reading printed books for thirty minutes a day prevents memory decline in older adults.`,`Screen reading causes faster memory decline than not reading at all.`,`Participants who read neither print nor screens declined faster than the screen readers.`,`The print readers spent less of their time on activities that harm memory.`,`The print readers' advantage would probably look larger if their age were accounted for.`],
      a:4, why:`Most strongly supported. The print readers started older, and older adults usually decline faster, yet they declined less. Take age into account and the gap should grow, not shrink. Prevents is far stronger than slightly less decline. Nothing compares the screen readers with the nonreaders, so neither claim about them has support. And the study recorded nothing about the rest of their time, so nothing supports a claim about how the print readers spent it.`, task:`LR10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`STAT`,`CAUSE`]}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Quarter to four, the door. Alma, and her grandmother in the good coat, for the four o'clock. Alma reached back in and hit the porch light, for later. Consuelo saw the car. One hand up, small, like at a bus stop. Dani put hers up too.`,
    `She didn't go home. Two miles, and she couldn't. Up Highway 2 to the refinery gate, turn around in the gravel, back down past the lot, up again. Radio off. Till the light went.`,
    `It was dark by the time she turned onto her own street, a little before six. The streetlight in front of the house had been flickering since Christmas, and in one of the flickers she saw it, and drove past her own driveway, and had to back up.`,
    `The washer was on the curb. The old one. Avocado green, the color of the whole house in 1990, sitting on a flattened box by the mailbox with its lid shut and its hoses coiled on top. A strip of duct tape across the front, in black marker, in capitals she'd know anywhere. LEAKS.`,
    `She went in through the side door and down the stairs without taking her coat off. The floor at the bottom was dry. It had been wet for a month and now it was dry, mopped, and there was a white washer where the green one had been, not new, a few years old, with a scuff on one corner and the hoses tightened down and the whole thing level. The shop vac was gone. On the lid, folded in a square, was one of his shop rags.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Teo" time="5:52 PM">that thing was leaking. dont make it a thing</prop>`,
    ((s.flags.ledconsuelo||G.ledconsuelo)
      ?`She read it standing in the basement. In her head Consuelo was still saying five ten, maybe five ten, trying the number out like a coat that didn't fit, a coat Dani had handed her. She read Teo's text again.`
      :`She read it standing in the basement. In her head Consuelo was still saying smaller, smaller than the big one, the only measurement she'd ever been willing to give. She read Teo's text again.`),
    `She went back out to the curb. Cold enough now that the box under it had frozen to the grass. Her hand on the lid. Fifteen, a Sunday, eight in the morning: this lid, this drum, a red sleeve stuck to the side of it. And later, in a room full of strangers, because it was true. In the washer, downstairs. It was wet. The cycle was done.`,
    `She lifted it. Empty. No, one sock. Hers, gray, plastered to the drum since January probably. Into the coat pocket.`,
    `Inside: the bathroom towels, the dish towels off the oven door, his rag. All of it in together. The dial turned the wrong way, the opposite of the old one, which cost her a minute and one bad word.`,
    `It filled quiet. The old one used to bang like somebody was locked in it. Two steps up from the bottom, her Wednesday step. She watched the concrete.`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`Phone out, after a while. Thank you, she typed, and looked at it, and killed it. Ok. Sent. Then the notebook on her knee and Consuelo's words, all of them, Consuelo's and not hers, the way Ana wanted, while the water ran.`
      :`Phone out, after a while. Thank you, she typed, and looked at it, and killed it. Ok. Sent. Consuelo's words were supposed to go down on paper tonight, every one. She sat on the step instead, and the water ran.`),
    `She stayed till the spin cycle. To be sure. Nothing came out from under it.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch05.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Rule`
});
